<h2>The prosecution case</h2>
<p>The allegation was that the appellant sexually assaulted an 11-year-old girl with a 40 per cent intellectual disability at her home on 28 May 2015. The FIR came ten days later, on 8 June 2015, after the child reportedly disclosed the incident to her teacher. The Special POCSO Court convicted the appellant under Section 6 — aggravated penetrative sexual assault — on 7 December 2021, sentencing him to ten years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment with a fine of Rs. 1 lakh, and the Meghalaya High Court dismissed his appeal in March 2022.</p>

<h2>Where the case unravelled</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Identification failed</h3><p>Neither the victim nor her cousin could identify the appellant in court, and both said his name had been supplied by the mother. Identity is the irreducible minimum of a criminal case.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Witness withheld</h3><p>The teacher to whom the child first disclosed the incident — the person whose report set the FIR in motion — was never examined. The Court drew an adverse inference against the prosecution.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Tutoring admitted</h3><p>The victim admitted that the Public Prosecutor had told her what to say — an admission that infected the only direct testimony in the case.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Medical evidence untested</h3><p>Two medical witnesses were not effectively cross-examined because defence counsel was absent; the Court held their testimony could not be relied upon in those circumstances.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Delay, omissions and the alibi</h2>
<p>The Court found the ten-day delay in lodging the FIR unexplained in material respects, with the FIR omitting key facts — including the claimed sighting of the appellant near the house. The mother&rsquo;s reaction to the information from her niece was found unnatural. The relationship between the appellant and the family was described inconsistently by the victim and the mother. And the defence evidence of alibi, the Court held, had been brushed aside without justification. The argument that the burden shifted to the accused under Section 106 of the Evidence Act failed because the &ldquo;last seen together&rdquo; circumstance was itself not established.</p>

<h2>The conclusion</h2>
<p>Allowing the appeal, the Bench of Justices Vikram Nath and Sandeep Mehta held that the prosecution had failed to establish guilt beyond reasonable doubt and that the appellant was entitled to be acquitted by giving him the benefit of doubt. Both judgments below were set aside and the appellant directed to be released forthwith if not wanted in any other case.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The judgment should not be read as diluting the protective architecture of the POCSO Act. It is, rather, a reminder that the Act&rsquo;s presumptions reward careful investigation and honest trial preparation — recording the first disclosure, examining the person who received it, conducting identification properly, and letting the child speak in her own words. Where the prosecution instead supplies the name, withholds the first-disclosure witness and leaves medical evidence untested, the special statute cannot save the case. For a child victim, a prosecution conducted this poorly is itself a failure of the system&rsquo;s duty toward her.</p>
</div>
